timestamp=2026-11-04T19:31:47.489Z level=INFO run=ebc6dc18 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="cat > /tmp/step_iqr.py << 'PY'\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\n\ndf = pd.read_csv('/app/dataset/synthetic_stroke_data.csv')\ndf = df.reset_index(drop=True)\nmask_bad = (\n    (df['avg_glucose_level'] < 20) | (df['avg_glucose_level'] > 800) |\n    (df['bmi'] < 10) | (df['bmi'] > 60) |\n    (df['age'] > 120)\n)\ndf_clean = df[~mask_bad].copy()\n\n# find all IQR outlier indices (by any numeric col) with glucose rule applied\nrejects = set()\n# compute bounds globally for clean df\nbounds = {}\nfor c in ['age', 'bmi', 'avg_glucose_level']:\n    q1 = df_clean[c].quantile(0.25); q3 = df_clean[c].quantile(0.75)\n    iqr = q3 - q1; lo = q1-3*iqr; hi = q3+3*iqr\n    bounds[c] = (lo, hi)\n\n# for each index, is it outlier by any?\nfor idx in df_clean.index:\n    is_out = False\n    out_by = []\n    for c in ['age', 'bmi', 'avg_glucose_level']:\n        lo, hi = bounds[c]\n        if (df_clean.loc[idx, c] < lo) or (df_clean.loc[idx, c] > hi):\n            is_out = True\n            out_by.append(c)\n    if not is_out:\n        continue\n    # apply glucose rule if outlier due to glucose\n    if 'avg_glucose_level' in out_by:\n        val = df_clean.loc[idx, 'avg_glucose_level']\n        # check rule on same values\n        same = df_clean[df_clean['avg_glucose_level'] == val]\n        total = len(same); stroke = same['stroke'].sum()\n        rate = stroke/total if total else 0\n        if rate >= 0.5:\n            continue  # do not reject\n        elif rate > 0.2:\n            # log2 transform and check again\n            val_t = np.log2(val) if val > 0 else val\n            same_t = df_clean.copy()\n            # find all with same val - transformed group is all observations that would have that transformed val?\n            # the rule says \"a base-2 logarithmic transformation should be applied and the above check should be performed again\"\n            # probably apply transformation to the variable globally or at least consider the distribution of transformed values? or just recompute the same-rate check on the group defined by original value but evaluated differently?\n            # Or more plausibly: transform avg_glucose_level for all observations (or just those in that group) and then see if the observation is still an outlier under the same criterion. But the rule says \"the above check should be performed again\" - \"above check\" is the 50%/20% check comparing stroke rates among observations sharing the same values.\n            # So after transformation, do we look at \"same values\" of the transformed variable? That changes the grouping.\n            # This is ambiguous. Common interpretation: for the outlying glucose values, if the cluster has >20% stroke rate (but <50%), transform glucose (log2) and re-assess outlier status? Maybe after transformation, the value is no longer an outlier by IQR. Or re-assess the same condition. Let us look at the full rule again.\n            # \"If more than 20% of other observations sharing the same values correspond to stroke cases, a base-2 logarithmic transformation should be applied and the above check should be performed again.\"\n            # \"above check\" refers to \"If 50% or more of other observations...\" (the preceding condition). So after transformation, check again: is 50%+ of others with same (transformed) values stroke cases? If yes, not considered outlier. If no (and maybe <=20% or between 20-50% still gives different result)? The rule as stated only gives two branches before transformation. After transformation, only \"the above check\" (the 50% one) is mentioned. Maybe if still not >=50% after transformation, then it IS considered an outlier? Or if after transformation the rate becomes <=20%, then also maybe treated as outlier? That’s unclear. But let us look at the actual data pattern.\n            pass\n    rejects.add(idx)\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
